A||N||6||reddog||A/ C blows strong and then weak||||1||
Z||000000||reddog||10-26-2001||08:31 PM||||I was just curious what would cause my A/C to blow strong around town but weak on long trips. It actually works very well during daily driving but I have noticed that when driving long distances It starts out strong and eventually reduces to blowing hardly at all. When it reduces you can see a cloud of vapor or mist coming from the vents. However, if I turn the A/C off for 10-15 minutes and then turn it back on it works perfect for a while but eventually does the same thing again. Any Ideas? Could it be the blower motor is overheating? I dunno!!!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Cutout_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ed||10-26-2001||09:36 PM||||The exchanger may be icing up.  Instead of the motor heating up, the exchanger is cooling down!  My car exhibited exactly the same symptoms.  The A/C is working so well that it is actually overcooling.<P>There's a valve, and unfortunately I've forgotten which one, which failed in my case.  It prevents the A/C from cooling too much, and cycles it to kept ice from forming on the exchanger.  The hardly blowing at all part is the exhanger fins filling up with ice and preventing the air from blowing through.  Turning off the A/C allows the ice to melt and you're back in business.<P>At least that's what happened to mine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-27-2001||01:01 PM||||Exactly what's wrong would depend on which AC compressor is involved... what year and motor are the car.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||reddog||10-27-2001||03:46 PM||||1986 GT 2.8 V6<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Cutout_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-28-2001||01:04 PM||||It could be simple high humidity.<P>Could be the drain is block. If it can't drain the water then it will leak on the floor and/or blow mist and/or freeze <P>Could also be over charged.<P>Posibly the pressure cycling switch has failed. Since that requires evacuating the system, replace the orifice tube at the same time.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||10-28-2001||01:10 PM||||If you turn the temperature up a few degrees you may eliminate the problem.Are you running the fan on high?The problem is you are refrigerating the evaporator coil faster than the air moved by the fan can remove it.You could also drive slower,NAH!||||reg||1
Z||000006||TooManyFieros||10-29-2001||08:38 AM||||PLEASE NOTE: Replacing the pressure switch does not require draining the system. There is a schrader valve on the accumulator under the switch. Just unscrew the old switch and and screw on the new switch. If nothing in your system has changed, I would go along with the switch sticking closed. The compressor is running all the time and freezing up the evap.Let us know what you find.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
